      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Formula:** $H_2O$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **State of Matter:** Water is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: \n  * **Liquid** (the water we drink, rivers, oceans)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, humidity in the air)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans maintain their chemical balance.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our body temperatures stable.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. That is why ice floats on water rather than sinking to the bottom, allowing marine life to survive winter under frozen lakes.\n\n### 3. The Ecological View (Earth’s Lifeblood)\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly recycled